-- title:
--   The two branch quotients of W[[u,v]]/(uv-π) are isomorphic
-- statement:
--   Let $W$ be a commutative ring and $\pi \in W$. Write $R_\pi =$ `UVCrossingModel W π` for the quotient of the two-variable power series ring $\mathrm{MvPowerSeries}(\mathrm{Fin}\,2, W)$ by the ideal generated by $X_0 X_1 - \pi$ (the constant series of $\pi$), and let `crossingSwap π` be the ring automorphism of $R_\pi$ induced by the automorphism of the power series ring that interchanges the two variables $X_0$ and $X_1$; this descends to the quotient because that substitution preserves the ideal generated by $X_0X_1 - \pi$. Let `U π` and `V π` be the two elements of $R_\pi$ designated by those names. The assertion is that there exists an isomorphism of rings $e \colon R_\pi/(U_\pi) \xrightarrow{\ \sim\ } R_\pi/(V_\pi)$ between the quotients by the principal ideals generated by `U π` and by `V π`, which is compatible with the two canonical surjections in the sense that for every $x \in R_\pi$ one has $e(x \bmod (U_\pi)) = (\mathrm{crossingSwap}\ \pi)(x) \bmod (V_\pi)$. Note that the statement asserts existence of such an $e$ rather than naming a particular isomorphism.
--
--   In the intended application $W$ is a complete discrete valuation ring and $\pi$ a uniformiser, so that $R_\pi$ is the local model for an ordinary double point and the two quotients are the two branches through it; the statement says that the involution interchanging the coordinates exchanges the two branches, so that anything proved for one branch transports to the other. It is used in the analysis of the two branch readings at a supersingular point, via [`IsLocalRing.exists_branchReadings_of_ringEquiv_adicCompletion_uvCrossingModel_pow`](thm.html#IsLocalRing.exists_branchReadings_of_ringEquiv_adicCompletion_uvCrossingModel_pow).
